# secret-scan-personal — operator-specific literals the secret guard should block.
#
# COPY this to the real, LOCAL path (never commit the real file — it holds the very data
# you are trying to keep out of repos):
#
#     cp tools/secret-guard/secret-scan-personal.example ~/.claude/secret-scan-personal
#
# secret-scan.sh reads ~/.claude/secret-scan-personal by default (override with
# $SECRET_SCAN_PERSONAL_FILE). It is machine-global — one file guards every repo.
# Absent default file → only key-shaped secret detection runs. A $SECRET_SCAN_PERSONAL_FILE you SET to a
# path that is not a regular file is an error (exit 2), not an opt-out; set it to /dev/null to opt out.
#
# Format: one ERE per line, matched CASE-INSENSITIVELY, over text AND decoded binary
# content (UTF-16/UTF-32). Blank lines and `# comments` are ignored.
#
# The rules the scanner enforces on this file (dir #695). A file it cannot trust FAILS the scan with exit 2
# and one reason line on stderr — it never scans on with fewer literals, because that would silently switch
# the personal half off:
#   - A trailing ` # note` is stripped (whitespace, then `#`), as is leading and trailing whitespace on every
#     line. To match a literal space at an edge, or a `#` after whitespace, write `[[:space:]]` or `[#]`.
#   - A UTF-8 byte-order mark on line 1 and a CR before each newline (a file saved on Windows) are ignored.
#   - A line that ENDS in a backslash is refused (an odd run of them): it would join the next line into one
#     pattern matching neither literal. Escape a dot as `\.` in the middle of a line, never at its end;
#     an even run (`\\` at the end, a literal backslash) is accepted.
#   - A line that is not a valid ERE is refused ("invalid regex"), e.g. an unclosed `(` or `[`.
#   - An unreadable file, or a symlink to nothing, is refused. $SECRET_SCAN_PERSONAL_FILE naming anything but
#     a regular file is refused too (see the top of this file).
# Where to see the reason: `secret-scan.sh --selftest` prints it, and so does install.sh's Verify, on the
# "secret-guard is wired but its selftest FAILS" line (`Reason:`).
#
# Keep each line a SIMPLE literal/character-class ERE. Avoid nested quantifiers
# (`(a+)+`, `(.*)*`) — on a backtracking grep (some BSD/busybox builds) a pathological
# pattern here can make the scan slow to a crawl on long input. Plain literals never backtrack.
#
# WHAT TO LIST: only literals that must NEVER appear in ANY repo. Keep each specific
# enough not to flag ordinary prose.
# WHAT NOT TO LIST: your bare home username (the `<name>` in /Users/<name>). It is a
# legitimate path component in a private knowledge base, so listing it would flag every
# home-path reference. Personal home paths in committed code should be caught by review,
# not this global file.
#
# Fill in YOUR real values below (this .example ships with placeholders only):
#
#   Real name(s) — full name and any handle that would personally identify you:
# Jane[[:space:]]+Q[[:space:]]+Public
#
#   Personal mounted-volume / backup-drive names (exact drive labels) — caught even
#   inside a UTF-16 binary fixture:
# My[[:space:]]?Backup[[:space:]]?Drive
#
#   Device serials (serials that must never ship in a fixture or a log):
# C[0-9A-Z]{10,11}
#
#   Personal email addresses:
# jane\.public@myisp\.example
